Ladybird’s worker process receives the User-Agent string, the
navigator compatibility mode, and the preferred languages from the
browser process, at launch and on every change. Blink hands a worker
its User-Agent at creation: WorkerGlobalScope stores
GlobalScopeCreationParams::user_agent, which the parent thread fills
from the document’s frame. WebKit does the same through
WorkerParameters::userAgent — Worker::create() passes
context->userAgent(), and WorkerGlobalScope keeps it as m_userAgent
for its WorkerNavigator. Gecko’s WorkerNavigator::GetUserAgent() asks
the main thread (GetUserAgentRunnable calls Navigator::GetUserAgent()),
and its languages are copied into the worker’s navigator properties by
RuntimeService::GetNavigatorProperties() from
Navigator::GetAcceptLanguages(), refreshed when the pref changes.
